   "body": "Update: while testing this on a dual-monitor setup I found a second code path the\r\noriginal fix did not cover, and I have pushed another commit for it.\r\n\r\nThe bug reproduces reliably when a second display is connected, and only\r\nintermittently on a single display. The reason is `ensureNoOverlap()`, which runs\r\nafter the work area clamp and undoes it:\r\n\r\n```ts\r\nwhile (existingWindowBounds.some(bounds => bounds.x === state.x || bounds.y === state.y)) {\r\n    state.x += 30;\r\n    state.y += 30;\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThree things combine here:\r\n\r\n1. The condition is `||`, so a match on x alone or y alone is enough to trigger an offset.\r\n2. It compares against every window on every display, not just windows on the target display.\r\n3. There is no re-clamping after the loop.\r\n\r\nDisplays arranged side by side share the same vertical origin and height, so every\r\ndefault-positioned window on either display computes the same y. A window on the\r\nfirst display therefore guarantees a y collision for a new window on the second, the\r\nloop runs repeatedly, and the accumulated offset pushes the title bar above the work\r\narea and the status bar behind the taskbar.\r\n\r\nOn my setup (two 1920x1080 panels at 125%, giving a logical work area of 1536x816) a\r\nworkspace window already starts at y = -18 before any offsetting, since the default\r\nheight of 900 exceeds the 816px work area. Each subsequent collision adds another 30px.\r\n\r\nThe new commit passes the work area into `ensureNoOverlap()` and stops offsetting once\r\nthe next step would push the window outside it. Behaviour is unchanged when there is no\r\ncollision. In the worst case a new window now overlaps an existing one instead of moving\r\noff screen, which seemed the right trade since the offsetting is cosmetic while staying\r\non screen is not.\r\n\r\nVerified locally on the setup described above, with the source window both maximized\r\nand not maximized.\r\n",
